1 NOTE The Vaccination Register, Milford Dispensary District, 1853-64 has 2 entries for Dillon’s from Kilmacrenan on 5 Sept 1856, one is James Dillon age 7 yrs and the other is Pat Dillon age 3 yrs. These 
2 CONC are most likely the children of Robert Dillon.
1 NOTE <p>Griffiths Valuation Record Information printed in 1858 shows Robert Dillon as follows:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>1. In the Townland of Massreagh, Parish of Kilmacrenan:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>a. The tenant an
2 CONC d occupier of a Flax Mill and Land, consisting of 2 pieces of land, one of 19 Acres - 0 Roods – 35                 Perches and the other 13 Acres – 0 Roods – 35 Perches with a total Annual Ratea
2 CONC ble Valuation of 16 Pounds                 and 5 Shillings.</p><p>b. The landlord of a house and garden, 10 Perches with a total Annual Rateable Valuation of 10 Shillings with                 Elizabet
2 CONC h Shiels as tenant.</p><p>c. The landlord of a house, office and land, 4 Acres - 1 Rood - 10 Perches with a total Annual Rateable Valuation                 of 1 Pound and 15 Shillings with John McDerm
2 CONC ott as tenant.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>2. In the Village of Kilmacrenan:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>a. The tenant and occupier of a house, offices, yard and sun garden (no acreage), with a total Annual Rateable   
2 CONC               Valuation of 7 Pounds.</p>
1 NOTE <p>I have researched the transport records and found a Robert Dillon that was sentenced to 7 years transport on 28 May 1844 in Dublin City for "obtaining money under forged orders". He was 35 years ol
2 CONC d at the time. The document reference number is "TR 5, P 60" and it can be viewed in Dublin at the National Archives</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Lastname:  DILLON</p><p>Firstname:  ROBERT</p><p>Sex:  M</p><p>A
2 CONC ge:  35</p><p>Trial place:  Dublin City</p><p>Trial date:  28/05/1844</p><p>Crime desc:  Obtaining money under forged orders</p><p>Sentence:  Transportation 7 yrs</p><p>Document ref1:  TR 5, P 60</p><
2 CONC p> </p><p>&nbsp;</p>
1 NOTE <p>Robert, and a brother bought two farms (cherry orchards) in the Kilmacrennan area.</p>Robert married Mary Sheils and it was her family that he bought the farm from.
1 NOTE <p>At one point, Robert (Thomas' father) went to the United Stated to work or find work, but he ended up coming back and staying on the farm. I don't know when this was or how long he was gone. While 
2 CONC he was in the USA Joe was looking out after the farm. Perhaps Edward was there helping out as well, because Edward must have had some sort of a share in the property at one time. Annette (Ivers) has a
2 CONC  copy of a hand written receipt signed by Edward stating he received 20 pounds from Robert as payment in full against any claim on the property of his late father (Thomas). It was dated Nov 1916. She 
2 CONC also had a hand written will leaving everything of Thomas' to Robert. That was dated 1913. So he had to have died between 1913 and 1916. Robert was the oldest son.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>From Tom and Mary
2 CONC : </p>Their father, Michael Joseph "Joe", told them that Robert (his grandfather; Thomas' great grandfather) had been transported to Australia, and he came into money while there. I believe they said 
2 CONC it was from mining of some sort. That is what enabled him to purchase his passage back to Ireland and gave him the funds to purchase the farm. They don't know anything about him having a brother, or a
2 CONC nother farm.
