1 NOTE <p>Notes for EDWARD (EDDIE DONIE) FRIEL:</p><p><p>Eddie had a shop in Ballymichael about 1912/13, as the late Mickey Mhiceal McGinley told me some years ago that he remembers his mother telling him th
2 CONC at she had been in Eddie Friel's shop on the day that he was born. The shop was located at the roadside, adjacent to the entrance to Donie's, on the site of the present "Teacher's house". It was sold 
2 CONC or converted to use as a hall and was used by the "Blueshirts" in the 1930s. It was the scene of a shooting at some time during its Blueshirt days, which led to intense local police activity. Eddie wa
2 CONC s not on the 1901 Census, but has reappeared by the 1911 Census, at which time he was noted as a farmer - his brothers were noted as labourers - which suggests that he was the "man of the house" in 19
2 CONC 11. Uncle John said that he died of TB and that, though he never got married, he was to have married Aunt Margaret's mother.</p><p><p>Eileen Carragher remembers her father (Francie) saying that Eddie 
2 CONC and James went to the US, but that only Eddie returned. However, she also stated subsequently that both Eddie and James returned and built the shop at Ballymichael together.</p><p><p>Eddie is also not
2 CONC ed starting in Ballymichael School on 4 February 1879 aged 3 and leaving in 1893.</p></p><p><p>Date of birth is from the LDS site.</p></p><p></p>
